      "content": "In the realm of database management systems, ensuring data integrity while allowing multiple users to access data simultaneously is a complex balancing act. This is primarily managed through **transaction isolation**, while **race conditions** and **deadlocks** represent the critical failures that occur when this balance is disrupted. Below is an explanation of these concepts, illustrated with practical examples.\n\n### 1. Transaction Isolation\n\n**Transaction isolation** is a property of database transactions that ensures concurrent execution of transactions does not interfere with each other. It defines the degree to which the operations of one transaction are visible to other transactions. The SQL standard defines four isolation levels, ranging from least to most restrictive:\n\n1.  **Read Uncommitted**: Transactions can see uncommitted changes from other transactions. This can lead to \"dirty reads.\"\n2.  **Read Committed**: Transactions can only see committed data. This prevents dirty reads but allows \"non-repeatable reads\" (where a row changes between two reads in the same transaction).\n3.  **Repeatable Read**: Ensures that if a transaction reads a row, it will see the same value if it reads it again. This prevents non-repeatable reads but may allow \"phantom reads\" (where new rows appear in a range query).",
